  "question": {
    "text": "Two stars of masses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>31</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> each, and at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>11</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> rotate in a plane about their common centre of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>.</mo></math> A meteorite passes through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> moving perpendicular to the stars' rotation plane. In order to escape from the gravitational field of this double star, the minimum speed that meteorite should have at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> is ( Take Gravitational constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.67</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>kg</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>)",
